### What is a load balancer?

A load balancer sits in front of N identical instances and, for each incoming request, selects one instance according to a configured algorithm. The goal is to spread work evenly so no instance is saturated while others are idle.

```java
package com.bank.loadbalancer;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/**
 * Simple round-robin load balancer for National Bank of India.
 * In production, use Kubernetes Service or Nginx/Envoy.
 */
public class RoundRobinBalancer {

    private final List<String> instances;
    private final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public RoundRobinBalancer(List<String> instances) {
        this.instances = instances;
    }

    public String nextInstance() {
        int index = counter.getAndIncrement() % instances.size();
        return instances.get(index);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var balancer = new RoundRobinBalancer(
            List.of("payment-1:8080", "payment-2:8080", "payment-3:8080"));

        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
            System.out.println("Request " + (i + 1) + " -> " + balancer.nextInstance());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Request 1 -> payment-1:8080
Request 2 -> payment-2:8080
Request 3 -> payment-3:8080
Request 4 -> payment-1:8080
Request 5 -> payment-2:8080
Request 6 -> payment-3:8080
```

### How does round-robin work?

Round-robin cycles through instances in order, giving each the next request in turn. It is fair when requests are uniform and instances are identical. Its weakness is that it ignores how busy each instance actually is.

```text
Round-robin with 3 instances:
Request 1 -> Instance A
Request 2 -> Instance B
Request 3 -> Instance C
Request 4 -> Instance A  (cycle repeats)

Problem: if Request 1 is a slow fraud check (5 seconds),
Instance A is still busy but round-robin sends Request 4 to it anyway.
```

### How does least-connections work?

Least-connections sends each new request to the instance currently handling the fewest active requests. This adapts to uneven request costs.

```java
package com.bank.loadbalancer;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class LeastConnectionsBalancer {

    private final List<String> instances;
    private final Map<String, Integer> activeConnections = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public LeastConnectionsBalancer(List<String> instances) {
        this.instances = instances;
        instances.forEach(i -> activeConnections.put(i, 0));
    }

    public synchronized String nextInstance() {
        return instances.stream()
            .min((a, b) -> activeConnections.get(a) - activeConnections.get(b))
            .orElseThrow();
    }

    public void onRequestStart(String instance) {
        activeConnections.merge(instance, 1, Integer::sum);
    }

    public void onRequestEnd(String instance) {
        activeConnections.merge(instance, -1, Integer::sum);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Instance A: 5 active connections (slow fraud checks)
Instance B: 1 active connection
Instance C: 2 active connections
Next request -> Instance B (fewest active)
```

### How does consistent hashing work?

Consistent hashing maps a key (like account ID) to an instance on a hash ring. The same key always hits the same instance, which helps local caching.

```java
package com.bank.loadbalancer;

import java.util.SortedMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class ConsistentHashBalancer {

    private final SortedMap<Integer, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();

    public void addInstance(String instance, int virtualNodes) {
        for (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; i++) {
            int hash = (instance + "-" + i).hashCode();
            ring.put(hash, instance);
        }
    }

    public String getInstanceFor(String accountId) {
        int hash = accountId.hashCode();
        SortedMap<Integer, String> tail = ring.tailMap(hash);
        int key = tail.isEmpty() ? ring.firstKey() : tail.firstKey();
        return ring.get(key);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var balancer = new ConsistentHashBalancer();
        balancer.addInstance("payment-1", 100);
        balancer.addInstance("payment-2", 100);
        balancer.addInstance("payment-3", 100);

        System.out.println("SAV2001 -> " + balancer.getInstanceFor("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("SAV2002 -> " + balancer.getInstanceFor("SAV2002"));
        System.out.println("SAV2001 -> " + balancer.getInstanceFor("SAV2001")); // same
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SAV2001 -> payment-2
SAV2002 -> payment-1
SAV2001 -> payment-2  (same instance every time for same key)
```

### How do health checks work?

Health checks periodically probe each instance and remove any that fail from the rotation. Without them, traffic would blindly include dead instances.

```yaml
# Kubernetes readiness probe for payment-service
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
spec:
  containers:
    - name: payment-service
      readinessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /actuator/health/readiness
          port: 8080
        initialDelaySeconds: 10
        periodSeconds: 5
        failureThreshold: 3
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Pod starts -> readiness probe fails for 10s -> NOT in Service endpoints
Readiness probe passes -> added to Service endpoints -> receives traffic
Pod crashes -> readiness fails 3 times -> removed from endpoints -> no traffic
```

### How does a Kubernetes Service distribute requests?

A Kubernetes Service provides a stable virtual IP and DNS name, and kube-proxy spreads requests across the healthy Pod endpoints.

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: payment-service
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP
```

```text
Expected behavior:
DNS: payment-service.default.svc.cluster.local -> 10.96.0.100 (virtual IP)
kube-proxy distributes to healthy pods:
  -> 10.244.0.5:8080 (pod 1)
  -> 10.244.0.6:8080 (pod 2)
  -> 10.244.0.7:8080 (pod 3)
Round-robin-style at the connection level.
```

### How do load balancing algorithms compare? (comparison table)

| Algorithm | Strategy | Best For | Weakness |
|---|---|---|---|
| Round-robin | Rotate in order | Uniform cheap requests | Ignores instance load |
| Least-connections | Fewest active requests | Variable request costs | Slightly more overhead |
| Weighted round-robin | Bias to capacity | Mixed-capacity fleet | Manual weight tuning |
| Consistent hashing | Key-based affinity | Caching, sticky sessions | Uneven if keys skewed |
| IP-hash | Client IP affinity | Session stickiness | Uneven with NAT |

### What should a senior engineer know?

kube-proxy balances TCP connections, not individual HTTP requests. For long-lived or HTTP/2 multiplexed connections, you need a layer-seven balancer or service mesh to truly balance request load.

```text
Layer 4 (kube-proxy): balances TCP connections
  Problem: one HTTP/2 connection carries 1000 requests to one pod
  Other pods sit idle.

Layer 7 (Envoy mesh): balances individual HTTP requests
  Fix: each request is independently routed to the least-busy pod.
```
